        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "cc537c77-12c7-5566-a4b1-54dbd730f5c3",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Kickapoo people, Cherokee Nation of Mexico, Anishinaabeg, Robert J. Conley\n- Places: Indiana, Mexico, Coahuila\n- Topics: Indian removals in Indiana, tribal recognition, indigenous groups, name meanings\n\nFacts:\n- Some Indian removals in Indiana occurred before 1830, but most removals in Indiana took place between 1830 and 1846.\n- The Kickapoo people are an Algonquian-speaking Native American tribe and an Indigenous Mexican tribe.\n- Anishinaabeg say that the name \"Kickapoo,\" rendered as \"Giiwigaabaw\" in Anishinaabe and cognate with the Kickapoo form \"Kiwikapawa,\" means \"Stands here and there.\"\n- Anishinaabeg say the meaning of the name \"Kickapoo\" may refer to the Kickapoo people's migratory patterns.\n- According to Robert J. Conley, the Cherokee Nation of Mexico is recognized by the state of Coahuila.\n- According to the Instituto Nacional de Lenguas Indígenas, the only indigenous people in Coahuila are the Kickapoo people.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "cd46e1a0-0e45-53ed-8b48-5899da673439",
        "text": "Page: Indian removals in Indiana. Sentence: Some of the removals occurred prior to 1830, but most took place between 1830 and 1846.\nPage: Kickapoo people. Sentence: The Kickapoo people (Kickapoo: Kiikaapoa or Kiikaapoi) are an Algonquian-speaking Native American and Indigenous Mexican tribe. \"\nPage: Kickapoo people. Sentence: Anishinaabeg\" say the name \"Kickapoo\" (\"Giiwigaabaw\" in the Anishinaabe language and its Kickapoo cognate \"Kiwikapawa\") means \"Stands here and there,\" which may have referred to the tribe's migratory patterns.\nPage: Cherokee Nation of Mexico. Sentence: According to Robert J. Conley, the Cherokee Nation of Mexico is recognized by the state of Coahuilla; however, according to the Instituto Nacional de Lenguas Indígenas, which manages the official surveys of indigenous groups in Mexico, the only indigenous peoples in Coahuila are the Kickapoo people.",
